---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PanelHead from '../../components/PanelHead.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Explain — Andrew Shiau"
  description="A skill that turns something I just worked out into an explainer with a diagram and a quiz that checks it landed."
>
  <!-- THE SPLIT (chooser round 2: concept 02, scope 03). Same `.pin` /
       `.splitrule` / `.feed` children at the same 4:8 columns as the index, so the
       site's one vertical division doesn't move when you click into a case study. -->
  <div class="grid">
    <PanelHead href="/work/explain/" title="Explain">
      <!-- TYPE B: no ledger in the panel. This page's facts block is a COLOPHON — what the
           thing is made of, read AFTER the work (STYLE.md) — so it stays at the foot of the
           reading column and the panel is marker + title + contact. The empty space under a
           three-item panel is the same condition the index's own panel is in; a ledger
           invented to fill it would be the one fabricated thing on the page. -->
    </PanelHead>

    <div class="feed">
      <!-- c1–12 of the FEED's twelve sub-tracks, not c1–8: the dek's own 32em cap is
           narrower than 8 of the feed's 700px, so c1–8 would set a column the text
           never reaches. No top margin — the dek is the top of the reading column. -->
      <div class="grid">
        <p class="dek c1-12">
          A skill that turns something I just worked out — an incident, a system, a recipe —
          into one self-contained explainer: a diagram, and a short quiz that checks whether
          the idea landed.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- TYPE B, beat 2: named head over the page's argument (was unheaded prose). -->
      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="shead">
          <h2>Why the quiz</h2>
          <div class="sbody">
            <p>
              Writing something down proves I can phrase it. It doesn’t tell me whether anyone
              understood it. Three questions a reader gets right does, so every explainer this
              skill writes ends in a quiz.
            </p>
            <!-- The "set in whichever house style suits the piece" clause is gone from here.
                 Two reasons, and they compound: it is no longer true — all three explainers fold
                 into this site's own document now (chooser: andrewshiau-grid-options, Q5/02), so
                 the claim is disproved one click away — and it was a verbatim repeat of the `Form`
                 ledger row further down this same page. A Type B page keeps its make-up in the
                 colophon, so the ledger is where that sentence belongs and this is a duplicate. -->
            <p>
              One prompt produces both halves, in one self-contained HTML file.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid">
        <div class="block c1-6">
          <span class="st">Both examples below are live.</span>
          <!-- "correct turns red" was wrong, and wrong in the one way this site can't afford:
               an explainer's accent has been the design navy (#14306b, #6ea8ff on dark) since
               the tokens were written, and it is measurably not red on either ground. A page
               arguing "verify against the real artifact" that mis-describes its own artifact
               loses the argument. What actually happens is described instead of named by
               colour, which is also the STYLE.md rule: colour is never the only signal. -->
          <span class="sd">Answer a quiz question — correct gets a ✓ and the accent rule, wrong gets an ✕.</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- The missing media (chooser round 2, Q3/03), as an EMBED rather than a recording.
           The block directly above says "both examples below are live" and then asks the
           reader to answer a quiz question — and until now the only thing below it was a
           links staircase, so the instruction pointed at nothing on the page. The quiz IS the
           claim on this page ("three questions a reader gets right"), and a quiz is the one
           artifact a recording actively weakens: watching me answer it proves nothing, and
           answering it in the frame is the whole test. So the frame is the evidence and it is
           also the interaction.
           The 10x essay, not canelé: its quiz is the one about the argument the site makes
           elsewhere, so a reader who answers it has read the thesis. `aping` embeds canelé —
           the two pages don't show the same page twice. -->
      <div class="grid" style="margin-top:calc(var(--unit)*1.5)">
        <figure class="embed c1-12">
          <div class="esrc">
            <a href="/writing/10x-is-a-loop/">open the explainer full-screen</a>
          </div>
          <div class="embed-frame">
            <iframe src="/writing/10x-is-a-loop/" title="An explainer with a diagram and a scored quiz, live"
              loading="lazy"></iframe>
          </div>
          <!-- The caption is a LABEL, not a second link (annotation: "still have double links, on
               top here and then on bottom, on most of these like embed links — should only have 1
               i think"). The `.esrc` above the frame keeps the link, because an embed is tall and
               the one actionable thing must not sit a screenful below it. Same fix as recipes. -->
          <figcaption>10x is a loop, not a prompt — the diagram and the quiz, live in the frame</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <div class="grid sect">
        <!-- a .links staircase follows, not a paragraph — head flush left -->
        <div class="c1-12"><h2>Explainers</h2></div>
      </div>
      <div class="grid" style="margin-top:var(--unit)">
        <div class="links">
          <a href="/writing/10x-is-a-loop/">
            10x is a loop, not a prompt <span class="arrowc">→</span>
            <span class="lu">AI-assisted engineering, from the public research</span>
          </a>
          <a href="/writing/canele/">
            What makes a canelé custardy <span class="arrowc">→</span>
            <span class="lu">one ratio, from six recipes normalised</span>
          </a>
          <a href="/writing/loops-vs-graphs/">
            Loops vs graphs <span class="arrowc">→</span>
            <span class="lu">agent architecture — when a loop should become a graph</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- TYPE B close: the facts colophon. -->
      <div class="grid sect">
        <div class="facts">
          <div class="frow">
            <div class="fm">Form</div>
            <div class="ftx">
              One prompt in, one self-contained HTML file out: the explanation, a diagram, and
              a scored quiz. The three on this site are set in the site's own system; the skill
              emits its own styles for anywhere else.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="frow">
            <div class="fm">The test</div>
            <div class="ftx">
              If a reader can’t pass the quiz, I rewrite the explainer. It’s the only check
              the skill makes.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
